# spark-update-check: weekly update and security report for Walter

This checker **only reports**. It never pulls images, upgrades packages or restarts
anything. All updates are manual and use the procedures below.

## What runs where

| Item | Location |
|---|---|
| Checker (python3 stdlib, root 0750) | `/usr/local/sbin/spark-update-check.py` |
| Schedule | `spark-update-check.timer`: Mon 09:00 UTC, `Persistent=true` (catches up after downtime) |
| Reports | `/var/lib/spark-update-check/report-YYYY-mm-dd.md`, with `latest.md` as a symlink to the newest |
| HTTP cache (ETag) | `/var/cache/spark-update-check/http/` |
| Login banner | `/etc/update-motd.d/90-spark-updates` (reads `latest.md`, no network) |
| Optional GitHub token | `/etc/spark-update-check/github-token` (root 0600, read-only PAT with no scopes). Not created by default. |
| Edge host (Covenant) | `~/.local/bin/spark-edge-check` on the **workstation**, run manually (see the end of this file) |

Commands:

```sh
sudo systemctl start spark-update-check.service     # run now
journalctl -u spark-update-check -n 20              # one-line summary per run
less /var/lib/spark-update-check/latest.md
sudo /usr/local/sbin/spark-update-check.py --stdout # print the report without writing files
# self-test of the advisory logic: pretend an older version is deployed
sudo /usr/local/sbin/spark-update-check.py --stdout --assume BerriAI/litellm=1.103.0 >/dev/null
```

Exit codes: `0` OK, `10` updates available, `20` security advisories affect current
versions, `1` checker error. The unit treats 10 and 20 as success, so only real errors
show the unit as failed.

### What is checked

* Every `image:` in `/srv/*/compose.yaml` (and `compose.yml` / `docker-compose.y*ml`),
  plus every digest-pinned image in `/etc/llama-swap/config.yaml`. New stacks are picked
  up automatically. For a digest-only ref (`repo@sha256:…`), the tag comes from a nearby
  comment such as `# postgres:17`. Keep those comments accurate.
* **Digest drift:** the registry (ghcr.io, Docker Hub, quay, nvcr; anonymous token flow)
  is asked what the pinned tag points to now. A difference means a newer build is
  available, for example a rebuilt `postgres:17` or `server-cuda`.
* **GitHub releases:** compares the latest stable release with the current version.
  llama.cpp uses `bNNNN` build tags, read from the image label
  `org.opencontainers.image.version`. The LiteLLM version is read from inside the pinned
  image (`docker run --network none`), which cross-checks the compose comment.
* **GitHub security advisories:** each one is evaluated against the current version
  using `vulnerable_version_range` and `patched_versions`. Backport lists are understood.
  An advisory whose range data cannot be compared (commit hashes, free text) is judged by
  its publication date against the current release date and marked "check manually".
  Some old advisories have incomplete data: an open-ended range with no patched version
  that predates the current release is marked "likely not affected (manual)".
* **hermes-agent:** the number of commits the local checkout is behind upstream
  `main`, and the latest release.
* **llama-swap:** `llama-swap --version` against the latest GitHub release.
* **OS:** `apt list --upgradable`, including how many updates come from `-security`.
  The package lists are refreshed by `apt-daily`; this checker does not run
  `apt update`. Also checked: unattended-upgrades status, reboot-required, held
  packages, the loaded NVIDIA driver against the apt candidate, and
  nvidia-container-toolkit and Docker versions.

Rate limits: anonymous GitHub API access allows 60 requests per hour. One run uses about
30–45. If the limit is hit, the checker retries with backoff, then falls back to the last
cached response (reported as such) or marks the check "unknown". Putting a token in
`/etc/spark-update-check/github-token` raises the limit to 5000 per hour.

