## How to update each component safely

General rules:

* Change one component at a time and verify it before moving on.
* Always pin by digest. Record the human-readable tag in the comment next to the digest,
  because the checker reads it.
* Back up the file first: `cp compose.yaml compose.yaml.bak-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S)`.
* **Rollback** = put the old digest back and run `docker compose up -d` again. Old
  images stay on disk until `docker image prune`, so do not prune until the new version
  has run for a while.
* Read the release notes for migrations, especially for LiteLLM and Open WebUI, which
  change their DB schemas. Downgrading after a schema migration may not work, so take a
  DB or volume backup first.

### Resolving a digest for a tag

```sh
docker pull ghcr.io/berriai/litellm:v1.104.0          # or postgres:17, etc.
docker image inspect --format '{{index .RepoDigests 0}}' ghcr.io/berriai/litellm:v1.104.0
# -> ghcr.io/berriai/litellm@sha256:<digest>   (use this in compose)
```

LiteLLM publishes cosign signatures. Verify them as you did for the current pin before
switching.

### LiteLLM and Postgres (`/srv/gateway`)

```sh
cd /srv/gateway
sudo cp compose.yaml compose.yaml.bak-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S)
# optional DB backup before LiteLLM upgrades (schema migrations run on start):
sudo docker compose exec db pg_dump -U litellm litellm | gzip > ~/litellm-$(date +%F).sql.gz
sudoedit compose.yaml        # update image digest + the "# ...:vX.Y.Z" comment
sudo docker compose pull litellm && sudo docker compose up -d litellm
sudo docker compose ps; sudo docker compose logs --tail 50 litellm
curl -s http://${BACKEND_WG_IP}:4000/health/liveliness
# then one real request through a harness (e.g. claude-spark / hermes-spark)
```

For Postgres, a new digest of the same `17` tag is a minor or patch rebuild:
`docker compose up -d db`, then wait for the healthcheck to report healthy. Never change
the major version (17 → 18) by editing the tag. That needs a dump and restore.

Rollback: restore `compose.yaml.bak-…` (or put the old digest back), then
`sudo docker compose up -d`.

### Open WebUI and Pocket-ID (`/srv/webui`)

```sh
cd /srv/webui
sudo cp compose.yaml compose.yaml.bak-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S)
sudoedit compose.yaml        # image: ghcr.io/open-webui/open-webui:vX.Y.Z@sha256:<digest>
sudo docker compose pull open-webui && sudo docker compose up -d open-webui
sudo docker compose logs --tail 50 open-webui
```

Verify: log in through `https://${SPARK_CHAT_HOST}` (Pocket-ID OIDC), list models,
and send one chat. For Pocket-ID, update `pocket-id` the same way and verify that login
at `https://${SPARK_ID_HOST}` works and that Open WebUI SSO still works. Back up the
named volumes before major versions:
`docker run --rm -v <vol>:/v -v $PWD:/b alpine tar czf /b/<vol>.tgz -C /v .`.

### llama.cpp server image (`/etc/llama-swap/config.yaml`, macro `image`)

```sh
docker pull ghcr.io/ggml-org/llama.cpp:server-cuda
docker image inspect --format '{{index .RepoDigests 0}} {{index .Config.Labels "org.opencontainers.image.version"}}' ghcr.io/ggml-org/llama.cpp:server-cuda
sudo cp /etc/llama-swap/config.yaml /etc/llama-swap/config.yaml.bak-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S)
sudoedit /etc/llama-swap/config.yaml   # macros.image: ghcr.io/ggml-org/llama.cpp:server-cuda@sha256:<new>
sudo systemctl restart llama-swap       # stops running model containers; next request reloads
journalctl -u llama-swap -n 50
```

Verify that each alias (coder, coder-fast, big, vision) loads and answers. Watch for
changed CLI flags in the release notes; `--fit` and `-fa` have changed before. Rollback
means putting the old digest back and restarting llama-swap.

The report shows "newer source builds" for llama.cpp when GitHub has newer `bNNNN`
releases than the image. This is informational: only a changed `server-cuda` digest
means a new image exists.

### llama-swap binary (`/usr/local/bin/llama-swap`)

Download the release tarball for linux_amd64 from GitHub `mostlygeek/llama-swap` and check
its checksum. Then:

```sh
sudo cp /usr/local/bin/llama-swap /usr/local/bin/llama-swap.v260   # keep the old one
sudo install -m 755 llama-swap /usr/local/bin/llama-swap
/usr/local/bin/llama-swap --version
sudo systemctl restart llama-swap && journalctl -u llama-swap -n 30
```

Rollback means copying `llama-swap.v260` back and restarting.

### hermes-agent (`~${BACKEND_SSH_USER}/.hermes/hermes-agent`, git checkout)

```sh
cd ~/.hermes/hermes-agent
git status                        # keep local changes safe (git stash) first
git fetch --tags origin
git log --oneline HEAD..origin/main | wc -l
git tag before-update-$(date +%F) # rollback point
git checkout v2026.9.24           # prefer a release tag over main
venv/bin/pip install -e .         # or the project's documented update step (hermes update / setup-hermes.sh)
hermes --version; hermes-spark    # quick smoke test
```

Rollback: `git checkout before-update-<date>` and reinstall.

### Monitoring stack (`/srv/monitoring`)

This works like the other compose stacks: update the digest and tag, run
`docker compose up -d <svc>`, then check that the Prometheus targets are up and that the
Grafana dashboards load. Rollback by putting the old digest back.

### Ubuntu packages and the NVIDIA driver

* Security updates are applied by **unattended-upgrades** (allowed origins: noble and
  noble-security). Any `-security` packages still pending are listed in the report. To
  apply them by hand: `sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade`, then check
  `/var/run/reboot-required`.
* **NVIDIA driver** (`nvidia-driver-580`): upgrade it deliberately, in a maintenance
  window, because it needs a DKMS rebuild and a reboot, and all GPU containers stop.
  Steps: `sudo apt install nvidia-driver-580 nvidia-utils-580`, then reboot, then run
  `nvidia-smi` and `docker run --rm --gpus all --runtime nvidia ghcr.io/ggml-org/llama.cpp:server-cuda --version`,
  then load one model through llama-swap. Keep the driver and `nvidia-container-toolkit`
  versions compatible. To roll back, install the previous version from the apt version
  table (`apt-cache policy nvidia-driver-580`, then
  `sudo apt install nvidia-driver-580=<old>`) and reboot. To pin a version:
  `sudo apt-mark hold nvidia-driver-580`.

